{%- comment -%}
  Get started: how to get each sim talking to Guido, one tab per sim.

  The steps are imperatives, and they follow the same order and the same voice as
  the setup wizard in the app, so a driver who reads this page and then opens
  Guido meets the list they already read.

  Tab state lives in Alpine because the marks at the top of the page link here by
  hash, and a hash that lands on #sim-lmu has to open the Le Mans Ultimate tab
  rather than leave Gran Turismo 7 showing. The tab buttons carry the ids those
  marks point at, so the browser scrolls to the strip on its own and the script
  only has to pick the tab.

  Every panel is in the markup and all but the first arrive with `hidden`, so with
  JavaScript off the section still reads: it shows GT7, the one sim that needs no
  second app on a PC.
{%- endcomment -%}
<section class="product-requirements" id="get-started">
  {%- comment -%}
    One anchor per sim, all of them parked on the section heading, so a mark at the
    top of the page lands on "Get started" rather than halfway down the section on
    the tab strip. The hash still names the sim, which is what the script below
    picks the tab up from; only where it lands changed. The tab buttons carry
    separate ids now, because an id is a single scroll target and these four have
    to sit above them.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- for sim in guidoSims.sims %}
    <span class="product-sim-anchor" id="sim-{{ sim.id }}" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  {%- endfor %}
  <h2 class="product-section-title">Get started</h2>
  <div
    class="product-sim-tabs"
    x-data="{
      tabs: [{% for sim in guidoSims.sims %}'{{ sim.id }}'{% unless forloop.last %}, {% endunless %}{% endfor %}],
      tab: '{{ guidoSims.sims.first.id }}',
      select(id) {
        this.tab = id;
      },
      move(step) {
        const next = this.tabs[(this.tabs.indexOf(this.tab) + step + this.tabs.length) % this.tabs.length];
        this.select(next);
        this.$nextTick(() => this.$refs[next]?.focus());
      },
      init() {
        const sync = () => {
          const hash = (location.hash || '').slice(1);
          if (!hash.startsWith('sim-')) return;
          const id = hash.slice(4);
          if (this.tabs.includes(id)) this.select(id);
        };
        sync();
        window.addEventListener('hashchange', sync);
      }
    }"
  >
    <div class="product-sim-tabs__list" role="tablist" aria-label="Choose your sim">
      {%- for sim in guidoSims.sims %}
        <button
          type="button"
          role="tab"
          id="sim-tab-{{ sim.id }}"
          x-ref="{{ sim.id }}"
          class="product-sim-tab"
          :class="{ 'is-active': tab === '{{ sim.id }}' }"
          :aria-selected="tab === '{{ sim.id }}'"
          :tabindex="tab === '{{ sim.id }}' ? 0 : -1"
          aria-controls="sim-panel-{{ sim.id }}"
          @click="select('{{ sim.id }}')"
          @keydown.arrow-right.prevent="move(1)"
          @keydown.arrow-left.prevent="move(-1)"
        >
          <span class="sim-mark sim-mark--{{ sim.mark }}" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <span class="product-sim-tab__name">{{ sim.name }}</span>
        </button>
      {%- endfor %}
    </div>
    {%- for sim in guidoSims.sims %}
      {%- comment -%}
        The steps ask for a console or a PC, and which one is the sim's own fact.
        Derived here rather than stored, so `relay` stays the single answer to
        "does this sim need Muretto".
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- assign sim_host = 'console' -%}
      {%- if sim.relay -%}
        {%- assign sim_host = 'PC' -%}
      {%- endif %}
      <div
        class="product-sim-panel"
        id="sim-panel-{{ sim.id }}"
        role="tabpanel"
        aria-labelledby="sim-tab-{{ sim.id }}"
        :hidden="tab !== '{{ sim.id }}'"
        {% unless forloop.first %}
          hidden
        {% endunless %}
      >
        <ul>
          {%- comment %}
            This logic is bound to break very soon, as it's the wrong level of abstraction. Better have the list
            stored in full in the data json, nevermind the duplication
          {%- endcomment %}
          {%- if sim.relay %}
            <li>
              Install and launch <a href="#muretto">Muretto</a> where {{ sim.name }} is installed.
            </li>
            <li>Start driving in {{ sim.name }}.</li>
          {%- else %}
            <li>Power on your PlayStation 5 and launch {{ sim.name }}.</li>
          {%- endif %}
          <li>Put your phone or tablet on the same home network as the {{ sim_host }}.</li>
          <li>Open Guido. It finds the {{ sim_host }} on its own.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    {%- endfor %}
    {%- comment -%}
      The one requirement that is the same whichever sim you picked, so it is said
      once here rather than four times inside the tabs.
    {%- endcomment %}
    <p class="product-sim-tabs__note">
      Guido runs on Android 7.0 or newer and on iOS 18.2 or newer.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
